## Recommended Stack

### Option 1 — GitHub Pages + Jekyll (Recommended for Full Control)

**What it gives you:**
- Free hosting on `https://mahfoud-amara.github.io/`
- Automatic conversion of Markdown files to a navigable website
- Custom themes and layouts
- No server required

**Setup steps:**
1. Create GitHub account and repository named `academic-portfolio`
2. Go to Settings → Pages → Source: Deploy from branch → `main` → `/root`
3. Add `_config.yml` (below) to the root of the repository
4. All `.md` files become web pages automatically

**Minimal `_config.yml`:**
```yaml
title: "Prof. Mahfoud Amara | Academic Portfolio"
description: "Sport, Politics, and Society in the Arab World"
author: "Mahfoud Amara"
theme: minima          # or "just-the-docs" for documentation style
plugins:
  - jekyll-feed
  - jekyll-seo-tag
```

**Recommended Jekyll themes for academic use:**
- `just-the-docs` — clean, with built-in search and navigation sidebar
- `minimal-mistakes` — highly customisable, supports author profiles
- `al-folio` — specifically designed for academics with publication lists

---

### Option 2 — GitHub Pages + MkDocs (Easier, More Modern)

**What it gives you:**
- Excellent search across all pages
- Clean, documentation-style layout
- `mkdocs.yml` for simple navigation configuration

**Setup:** Add `mkdocs.yml` and deploy with GitHub Actions.

---

### Option 3 — Static HTML/CSS (Maximum Design Control)

For the interactive timeline, a single `index.html` file with embedded CSS and JavaScript 
provides full control. This is the approach described in the HTML prototype below.

---

## Interactive Features Compatible with GitHub Pages

### 1. Chronological Timeline (JavaScript)
A vertical or horizontal scrollable timeline using pure HTML/CSS/JS. Works without any 
server-side code. Key works are plotted as nodes; clicking a node opens the work's page.

**Libraries to use (CDN, no installation):**
- `TimelineJS3` (Northwestern University Knight Lab) — embeds via iframe
- `vis-timeline` — highly customisable JS library

### 2. Tag/Theme Filtering
A simple JavaScript filter on the works index page: users click tags like `#gender`, 
`#olympics`, `#MENA` to filter the list of works.

```html
<!-- Example filter button -->
<button onclick="filterWorks('gender')" class="tag-btn">Gender & Identity</button>
```

### 3. Cross-Work Relationship Map (Optional, Advanced)
Use `D3.js` (available via CDN) to render a force-directed network graph showing 
connections between works based on shared themes. Each node = one work; edges = 
shared theoretical frameworks.

### 4. Embedded Citation Generator
A `<button>` on each work page that copies the formatted citation (Chicago, APA, 
or MLA) to the clipboard.

```javascript
function copyCitation() {
  navigator.clipboard.writeText(document.getElementById('citation').innerText);
  alert('Citation copied!');
}
```

### 5. Bilingual Support (Arabic/English)
GitHub Pages supports RTL text via CSS (`direction: rtl`). A toggle button can 
switch the page between English and Arabic versions.

